#4471 was merged by subject line ([decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject)), so it
swept up every "wrong day" report whatever caused it. Nobody read the bodies. Run `causes.ts`
over the 412 deduped reports (427 rows, 15 are duplicate ids from overlapping export pages) and
they split by tenant locale and booking form into **two** distinct problems, not one.

<Callout>
Two causes, two tickets. #4471 is really the parser bug; the form-hint bug rode in on a matching
subject line and has no ticket of its own.
</Callout>

## What #4471 actually holds

<CauseTable ticket={4471} />

## The two problems

**1. The date parser reads month-first (379 reports).** The shared parser reads every date month
first. All eleven tenants run day-first (European) locales, so a date like 7/4 (7 April) comes
back as 4 July. This is the real #4471. It covers every channel (app 204, email 150, form 25) and
every tenant, seen 2026-04-01 through 2026-10-04. Filed as [parser · 379 reports](https://github.com/greensteph/support-kb-4471/issues/1).

**2. The booking-v3 form hint says MM/DD/YYYY (33 reports).** Three tenants (alder.physio,
copper.studio, pale.harbour) run the `booking-v3` form, whose date field prints a `MM/DD/YYYY`
hint to day-first users. They type month-first and the form stores it that way: a different bug,
in the form, not the parser. Form channel only, seen 2026-04-17 through 2026-09-22. Filed as
[hint · 33 reports](https://github.com/greensteph/support-kb-4471/issues/2).

Intake's `intakeTag` guessed wrong both ways: it tagged 23 parser reports as `hint` and 15 hint
reports as `parser`. The cause is the tenant's locale and form, not intake's guess at the
browser.

## One-day move (a footnote, not a third ticket)

`desk-notes.json` flags five reports that read as a one-day shift rather than a month flip
(for example "client in Lisbon got Tuesday, client in Toronto got Monday, same meeting"). The
desk counts them under `parser` by tenant and footnotes them, so they stay inside the 379 above.
Worth a second look as a possible timezone or off-by-one bug, but not a separate problem in
#4471 today.

